NASA material (public; credit NASA)
- NASA Earth Information Center daily visualizations, NASA Scientific
  Visualization Studio: SVS 5149 (precipitation), 5151 (PM2.5), 5147
  (near-surface temperature). Frames and the grids decoded from them are in
  app/public/data/.
- NASA GEOS-CF v2 surface PM2.5 (pm25_rh35), NASA GMAO, read from NCCS OPeNDAP.
- NASA GISS Surface Temperature Analysis (GISTEMP v4).
- NASA Visible Earth: Blue Marble and Black Marble (app/public/tex/).
- NASA SVS 5313, Change in Night Lights between 2012 and 2023 - EIC Version
  (Black Marble, NASA/NOAA Suomi NPP VIIRS): app/public/data/lights/.
- NASA SVS 5011, Lightning Events Detected from the International Space Station
  (ISS) 2017-2023 (LIS on the ISS, NASA GHRC DAAC): app/public/data/lightning/.
- International Space Station predicted trajectory (CCSDS OEM), NASA Johnson
  Space Center, Flight Operations Directorate (TOPO): app/public/data/station.json.

Other material
- Coastlines: Natural Earth via world-atlas (public domain; package ISC).
- Sounds of a home (app/public/home-sounds/): public-domain recordings from
  Wikimedia Commons; author, source and changes for each clip are in
  app/public/home-sounds/manifest.json. No clip contains a person's voice.
- Libraries: three.js (MIT), d3-geo (ISC), topojson-client (ISC), Vite (MIT),
  Vitest (MIT), Playwright (Apache-2.0), axe-core (MPL-2.0), TypeScript
  (Apache-2.0); numpy (BSD-3-Clause), Pillow (MIT-CMU), requests (Apache-2.0),
  netCDF4 (MIT), reportlab (BSD-3-Clause).
- Fonts, loaded from Google Fonts: Archivo and Noto Sans SC (SIL Open Font
  License 1.1).

Use of AI
Most of the code, tests and documentation were written with an AI coding
assistant, directed and reviewed by the team; many commits in the git history
carry a Co-Authored-By trailer. The sounds are synthesized at run
time by app/src/audio/engine.ts from NASA data, the home sounds are
recordings made by people, and the globe pictures are NASA's. The team
poster, demo videos and project document were assembled with AI assistance
from screen recordings of the app and carry a visible "AI-assisted (SI)" mark.
